{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789492520,"nonce":"B1PKxg41AeSFHGjLeRWLZ2WrHZU=","title":"The warm-up thread starves first calls","message":"The once-only table of first-call compiles was a mutex held for the whole compile, and the warm-up thread took it back the moment it released it, so a first call from the program waited through every compile the warm-up had queued before its own: bench_closure_call and bench_lambda_call ran 8 to 14 ms, the promotion of their loop arriving 8 ms late.\n\nNow the table holds the entry or the mark of a compile under way (a second call for the same function waits on a condvar), so different functions compile side by side under the backend's own lock, and the warm-up thread stands aside while a first call is waiting to compile. Measured on the M1 Pro: closure_call and lambda_call 7.8 ms with the warm-up on, 8.0 with it off; bench_pipeline exec 54 to 57 off, 45 to 51 on; bench_tokenize 80 off, 70 to 75 on.","files":null}]}